When would you use Kubernetes?

For example, imagine a large college system:

                    Users
                      ↓
                 Kubernetes ☸️
                      │
     ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
     ↓                ↓                ↓
AI Chatbot      Student Portal    Admission System
Containers      Containers        Containers
     ↓                ↓                ↓
Database         Database          Database


Kubernetes becomes useful when you have many services, containers, replicas, and more advanced infrastructure requirements.

======

| Technology           | Simple Meaning                                        | Your Project           |
| -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- |
| **Containerization** | Package app and dependencies together                 | ✅ Used                 |
| **Docker**           | Build/package containers                              | ✅ Used                 |
| **Docker Image**     | Packaged application template                         | ✅ Used in deployment   |
| **Docker Container** | Running application package                           | ✅ Used                 |
| **Cloud Run**        | Managed platform that runs your container             | ✅ Used                 |
| **Serverless**       | Provider manages the underlying infrastructure        | ✅ Used                 |
| **Kubernetes**       | Manages and orchestrates container workloads at scale | ❌ Not currently needed |


=========

Containerization = The process 📦
Docker = The tool 🐳
Container = The running package 🚀
Kubernetes = The manager of containers ☸️
Cloud Run = Managed/serverless platform to run your container ☁️

========

For your College AI Assistant, your current setup is a good example of:

FastAPI Application → Docker Container → Google Cloud Run → Serverless Deployment → Vertex AI

For your College AI Assistant, the key distinction is that 
Docker creates the container, while Kubernetes manages multiple running containers when scaling and reliability become more complex.

